      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to put together a travel compliance checklist for a group of 12 engineers heading through Singapore, Vietnam, and Thailand next month. I'm getting really confused with the difference between transit visas and full entry visas for the layovers in certain zones. Can you give me a breakdown of what we need to look out for so nobody gets stuck at immigration or detained at a border?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'd be happy to help you with your travel compliance checklist. For a group of 12 engineers with layovers in Singapore, Vietnam, and Thailand, here's a brief overview. \n\nFor Singapore, you'll need to ensure everyone has a valid passport with at least two blank pages for visa stamps. As a transit point, no visa is required for most nationalities, but it's always best to check with the Singaporean embassy or consulate. \n\nFor Vietnam, you'll need to show proof of onward travel and sufficient funds, and visa requirements vary depending on the nationality and purpose of the trip. For a layover, you'll likely need an e-visa or a visa on arrival. You can check the Vietnamese government's website for specific requirements.\n\nFor Thailand, you'll need a valid passport and visa, which usually requires an e-visa or visa on arrival for touristic purposes. If you're staying longer than 30 days, you'll need to apply for a long-stay visa in advance.\n\nIt's always a good idea to verify with each country's embassy or consulate for the most up-to-date information and requirements for your group's specific travel plans.",
